// gserver.sla -- the goroutine concurrency model, selected by
// SLAREDIS_LOOP_MODEL=goroutine (see main.sla); the poll model
// in server.sla remains selectable as the rollback shape
// (PLAN-GOROUTINE section 5.4).
//
// Shape (PLAN-GOROUTINE sections 2-3, Jev ruling
// executor_goroutine 0.97): one goroutine per connection; a
// single executor goroutine owns the store and runs every
// command batch it receives over one FIFO channel; a
// dedicated AOF writer goroutine owns the AofState; a
// dedicated sweeper goroutine posts expiry sweeps to the
// executor; a supervisor goroutine watches all of them. All
// socket IO is concentrated in one driver, which runs on the
// main goroutine's frame (see the IoSt comment for why), and
// hands bytes to and from the connection goroutines over
// channels; a loopback wake socket lets producers pull the
// driver out of its readiness wait immediately.
//
// Why not the poll shape: the poll loop's connection table
// forced every connection's buffers through one shared Vec,
// and its periodic reap rebuilt that table by moving live
// connections' state -- the shape behind the
// >=1000-connection bulk-close crash (delivery record,
// face 4). Here a connection's state lives in its own heap
// job block, its lifetime is its goroutine's lifetime, and
// retirement is a pointer swap in the driver's slot table
// plus a monitor message: no connection's bytes are ever
// rebuilt because another connection ended. There is no
// reap path at all. Blocking commands are plain sequential
// code inside a connection goroutine; no readiness
// bookkeeping exists outside the driver.
//
// Redis serial semantics are preserved by construction:
// commands execute atomically one at a time in a definable
// global order. Since round-2 step 3 (W1 full inline),
// commands normally execute in the connection goroutine
// under the store mutex (g_try_inline), reads and writes
// alike; the global order is the interleaving of mutex
// acquisitions with the executor's batch order. The executor
// still runs the fallback batches (a pass that misses the
// try-lock, or starts with a pending batch, rides the batch
// whole -- so per-connection order stays exact), the sweeps,
// the serializations, and BGREWRITEAOF's dance. Replies are
// produced in command order and the connection goroutine
// appends them to its out buffer in that order, interleaved
// correctly with its locally handled AUTH traffic (a pending
// batch is always flushed before a local AUTH step runs).
// AOF record order equals execution order by construction:
// an inline write sends its append op on aof_chan while
// still holding the store mutex, so op sends serialize in
// exactly the order the writes executed; an executor batch's
// records are likewise sent only by the executor, after its
// own in-lock executions of that batch.
//
// Ownership and lifetime rules (all shapes probe-verified
// against the master toolchain; see
// docs/GOROUTINE_DELIVERY.md for the full defect register):
//   - Cross-goroutine sharing goes through Box'd heap blocks
//     whose pointers travel over channels; fields are read
//     and written through the pointer directly. Borrows
//     never cross a spawn (the checker rejects them).
//   - A &(*p) borrow of a boxed VALUE struct is not a borrow
//     of the heap object on this toolchain: scalar fields
//     read wrong and callee writes land elsewhere (measured
//     on AuthConfig and AofState). The sound form, used
//     everywhere here, is an OWNED VALUE FIELD inside a heap
//     block, borrowed in place: &(*job).auth_cfg,
//     &(*job).wst, &(*ctx).store. Frame locals of a goroutine
//     (values and borrows alike) are likewise not trusted
//     across a park: all cross-cycle state lives in the job
//     blocks, re-derived from the job pointer at each use.
//   - Binding a Vec field of a heap struct with `let x =
//     (*p).field` MOVES it out (that is the codebase take
//     idiom); on shared structs it must be read in place
//     element-wise instead (measured: a cloned password was
//     drained by the first connection's clone).
//   - CmdReq blocks are per-sender and reused: a connection
//     owns exactly one, has at most one batch outstanding,
//     and reuses the block for its whole lifetime.
//   - AofOp blocks come from a SrvCtx-owned pool of 256,
//     taken and returned under the op mutex (round-2 step 3;
//     the pool lived in the executor before). Completion is
//     per-op: the writer signals the op's done channel
//     (waited appends and installs), returns it on the
//     recycle channel (executor group-commit deferrals), or
//     returns it to the pool itself (fire-and-forget ops) --
//     exactly one of the three, exactly once per dispatch.
//   - A connection's CmdReq/ConnJob blocks are retained for
//     the process lifetime (two small blocks per connection
//     ever served). Documented here rather than hidden: the
//     connection goroutine cannot free its own job block,
//     and no verified free-handoff shape exists yet.
//   - Hot tables (driver slots, supervisor monitors) are
//     fixed slot arrays with index assignment, never Vec
//     fields pushed from a loop (measured defect, probes
//     18f/18h in the delivery record).
//   - CHANNEL-OP DISCIPLINE (measured, probes 27/28/30/31):
//     a channel operation (send / recv / select) written
//     textually inside a never-returning loop body leaks
//     fixed-stack space on EVERY iteration -- a plain recv
//     in a loop body overflows the 256 KiB goroutine stack
//     within thousands of iterations; a select leaks in
//     proportion to its arm count. The same operations
//     inside a CALLED function rewind on return (300k calls
//     measured clean). Therefore every long-lived goroutine
//     in this module is a thin loop whose body is a single
//     call to a step function that performs the cycle's
//     channel work and returns.
//
// Yield discipline (the compiler inserts no yield points):
// every CPU loop this module owns -- the executor's batch
// walk and the connection parse walk -- calls
// sa_task_yield() every yield_every commands
// (SLAREDIS_YIELD_EVERY, default 128; 0 disables). Loops
// that block on channels park through the runtime and need
// no manual yield.
//
// Supervision (PLAN-GOROUTINE section 3.6): the supervisor
// monitors the executor, the writer, the sweeper, and every
// connection goroutine. A connection exit with a non-zero
// code closes only that connection (the supervisor asks the
// driver to close the stream; the trap is counted). The
// sweeper is respawned on exit. The executor and the AOF
// writer are single points by design: if either exits, for
// any reason, the supervisor raises ctx.core_dead and the
// driver returns exit code 71 -- a loud process failure
// recovered by AOF replay on restart, never a silent solo
// restart that would serve a half-owned dataset.
@import "sa_std/net.sai"
@import "sa_std/net.sla"
@import "sa_std/time.sai"
@import "sa_std/fs.sai"
@import "sa_std/status.sla"
@import "sa_std/vec.sa"
@import "sa_std/ptr.sa"
@import "sa_std/string.sa"
@import "sa_std/task.sai"
@import "sa_std/core/box.sa"
@import "sa_std/core/slice.sa"
@import "bytes.sla"
@import "store.sla"
@import "resp.sla"
@import "commands.sla"
@import "auth.sla"
@import "journal.sla"
@import "aof.sla"

const CMD_BATCH: u8 = 0;
const CMD_SWEEP: u8 = 1;
const CMD_SERIALIZE: u8 = 2;

const AOF_OP_APPEND: u8 = 0;
const AOF_OP_INSTALL: u8 = 1;

const IO_OUT_BYTES: u8 = 0;
const IO_OUT_CLOSE_AFTER_FLUSH: u8 = 1;
const IO_OUT_CLOSE_NOW: u8 = 2;

// Exit code when a core goroutine (executor / AOF writer /
// listener) dies under the supervisor; see the header comment.
const G_EXIT_CORE_DEAD: i32 = 71;

// Inline-write wait bound (round-2 step 3): a connection pass
// holds at most this many fsync=always append ops awaiting
// their covering sync before it waits mid-pass (bounds
// per-connection op holdings; see g_inline_wait).
const INL_WAIT_CAP: u64 = 64;

struct SrvCtx {
    auth: *AuthConfig,
    aof_enabled: bool,
    aof_fsync: u8,
    // The dataset, as an owned value field of this heap block
    // (the sound borrow shape, &(*ctx).store), and the mutex
    // that serializes every access to it (round-2 step 1).
    // The executor used to be the store's only owner; since
    // steps 1 and 3, read and write commands also execute in
    // connection goroutines under this mutex (see
    // g_try_inline). The mutex is held around pure store work
    // plus exactly one channel operation: an inline write's
    // append-op send on aof_chan, whose boundedness argument
    // is recorded at g_try_inline_write (the writer drains
    // the channel without ever needing this mutex and the
    // channel is buffered, so the stall is at most one writer
    // group cycle).
    store: Store,
    store_mu: u64,
    // The AOF op pool (round-2 step 3, moved up from the
    // executor): 256 preallocated blocks shared by every op
    // producer -- connection goroutines dispatching inline
    // appends, the executor's batch and install traffic.
    // Guarded by op_mu, whose critical section is a single
    // Vec tail take or append: nanoseconds, never held across
    // a park. Lock order is store_mu -> op_mu at most, and in
    // practice the two never nest (pool takes happen before
    // the store lock is acquired or after it is released; the
    // one in-lock operation, the append send, does not touch
    // the pool) -- so the pool cannot become a serialisation
    // point next to the store mutex, whose sections are whole
    // command executions.
    op_pool: Vec<*AofOp>,
    op_mu: u64,
    cmd_chan: Chan<*CmdReq>,
    aof_chan: Chan<*AofOp>,
    aof_recycle: Chan<*AofOp>,
    sup_reg: Chan<(u64, u64)>,
    // Connection goroutines hand reply bytes and close
    // requests to the IO driver on this channel, then poke the
    // driver's wake socket (wake_tx) so its poll wakes at once.
    io_out: Chan<*IoOut>,
    wake_tx: u64,
    // Set by the supervisor goroutine when a core goroutine
    // (executor / AOF writer) dies; the IO driver runs on the
    // main goroutine's frame and checks this once per round,
    // then returns G_EXIT_CORE_DEAD. (A plain bool written by
    // one goroutine and read by another; the scheduler's
    // park/wake edges order the accesses in practice, the
    // same assumption the sticky aof_broken flag relies on.)
    core_dead: bool,
    // Sticky failure flag: written only by the AOF writer
    // goroutine (the AofState owner), read by the executor at
    // batch boundaries and by connection goroutines before an
    // inline write. Monotonic (false -> true only), so the
    // unsynchronized read can lag a command but never invent
    // a recovery that did not happen; under fsync=always,
    // waiters (the executor for its synchronous appends and
    // installs, a connection for its inline appends)
    // additionally learn failures synchronously from the op
    // result they wait for.
    aof_broken: bool,
    yield_every: u64,
}

struct CmdReq {
    kind: u8,
    // true: the sender blocks on done (connection batches).
    // false: completion is signalled best-effort (sweeper and
    // writer requests; nobody waits, so the signal must never
    // block the executor).
    ack: bool,
    batch: Vec<Vec<Vec<u8>>>,
    replies: Vec<Vec<u8>>,
    done: Chan<u64>,
}

struct AofOp {
    kind: u8,
    idx: u64,
    chunks: Vec<Vec<u8>>,
    data: Vec<u8>,
    result: bool,
    // Completion protocol (round-2 step 3): the dispatcher
    // arms exactly one of these before sending the op, and
    // the writer completes the op exactly once, after the
    // group's write (and, under fsync=always, its covering
    // sync) has landed or failed:
    //   wait: the writer signals done (cap 1) with result
    //         set; the dispatcher waits on it and returns the
    //         op to the pool itself. Used by every
    //         fsync=always append not on the group-commit
    //         path and by waited installs.
    //   via_recycle: the writer returns the op on
    //         aof_recycle; the executor's group-commit
    //         pending FIFO consumes it (g_recycle_handle).
    //   neither: fire-and-forget; the writer returns the op
    //         to the SrvCtx pool when done with it.
    // A wait op's done channel is drained exactly once by
    // its waiter, so no stale token can survive into a later
    // use of the block.
    done: Chan<u64>,
    wait: bool,
    via_recycle: bool,
}

// IO driver -> connection goroutine: one read chunk (up to
// 4 KiB) or the end-of-stream marker.
struct IoMsg {
    bytes: Vec<u8>,
    eof: bool,
}

// Connection goroutine -> IO driver, on the shared io_out
// channel: reply bytes to append to the connection's out
// queue, or a close request (IO_OUT_CLOSE_AFTER_FLUSH: close
// once the queue has drained; IO_OUT_CLOSE_NOW: close at
// once, used by the supervisor for trapped connections).
struct IoOut {
    fd: u64,
    kind: u8,
    bytes: Vec<u8>,
}

struct ConnJob {
    ctx: *SrvCtx,
    // This connection's own copy of the auth config (cloned
    // by the IO driver at accept time). It must be an owned
    // field, not a borrow of the shared config: &(*p) on an
    // already-boxed value struct yields a borrow of a shallow
    // temporary on this toolchain (measured: the scalar
    // `required` and the password header read back wrong
    // inside connection goroutines, silently disabling AUTH;
    // delivery record), while a borrow of a heap-resident
    // field -- &(*job).auth_cfg, the same shape as the
    // resp_parse borrow of inbuf -- is sound.
    auth_cfg: AuthConfig,
    stream: u64,
    req: *CmdReq,
    inbox: Chan<*IoMsg>,
    // The whole per-connection state lives in this heap block,
    // not in the goroutine's frame: frame values held across a
    // park (chan send/recv, net wait) are not reliable in this
    // toolchain (measured: after the first dispatch cycle a
    // connection's frame Vec headers read back corrupted --
    // stale-buffer replays and zeroed reply buffers). Heap
    // fields accessed through the job pointer are the shape
    // every working goroutine program in this stack uses.
    inbuf: Vec<u8>,
    inoff: u64,
    outbuf: Vec<u8>,
    // Retained but unused since the miss-batch-fallback
    // reshaping of g_try_inline: this slot held the cloned
    // arguments for the first inline form's contended path
    // (park on the blocking lock, execute off the slot). That
    // shape is gone -- a try-lock miss now rides the batch --
    // and the field stays only so this reshaping remains
    // confined to the fallback branch; removing it (and its
    // constructor initialiser) is a separate cleanup.
    inl_args: Vec<Vec<u8>>,
    // Inline-write sync waits (round-2 step 3): this pass's
    // append ops dispatched under fsync=always, in send
    // order. Their done channels are drained (and the ops
    // returned to the pool) before the pass's replies are
    // emitted -- g_inline_wait runs ahead of the pass-end
    // batch flush, and INL_WAIT_CAP triggers the same wait
    // mid-pass. Empty under every other fsync mode (those
    // ops are fire-and-forget; the writer returns them).
    inl_ops: Vec<*AofOp>,
    // Latched when one of this connection's own append ops
    // reports failure. The writer also raises the ctx-wide
    // sticky flag (aof_broken); this per-connection latch is
    // the synchronous, exact half for the connection that
    // paid for the failed sync. While set, writes are
    // refused with the AOF error, mirroring the executor's
    // broken latch.
    aof_dead: bool,
    authed: bool,
    close_after_flush: bool,
    yield_ct: u64,
    auth_ok_pending: bool,
}

struct ExecJob {
    ctx: *SrvCtx,
}

struct AofJob {
    ctx: *SrvCtx,
    // The AOF state as an owned value field (see the
    // ConnJob.auth_cfg comment: borrows of an already-boxed
    // value struct, &(*p), are unreliable in goroutine code on
    // this toolchain -- the writer's flush silently operated
    // on a phantom copy and the file stayed empty -- while a
    // borrow of a heap-resident field is sound). The writer
    // goroutine is the state's only owner.
    wst: AofState,
    // Step 2 group-write merge buffer: each drained group's
    // record chunks are appended here (bytes_push_range
    // accumulation is linear on this toolchain -- probe_gbuf:
    // 0.11-0.19 us/byte across 26x80 B, 256x80 B and 8x64 KiB
    // shapes) and written with one aof_write_group call.
    gbuf: Vec<u8>,
    sreq: *CmdReq,
    rw_out: bool,
}

struct SweepJob {
    ctx: *SrvCtx,
    req: *CmdReq,
}

struct IoJob {
    ctx: *SrvCtx,
    listener: u64,
    wake_rx: u64,
}

// The IO driver's per-connection socket state. The table holds
// pointers only, so retiring a connection never moves another
// connection's data (the face-4 lesson); the byte queue lives
// in the heap block.
struct DriverConn {
    fd: u64,
    inbox: Chan<*IoMsg>,
    outq: Vec<u8>,
    out_off: u64,
    ready: u32,
    read_closed: bool,
    eof_sent: bool,
    close_after_flush: bool,
    dead: bool,
}

// Supervisor state (heap block, like every other goroutine's:
// the registration vectors are read and mutated across the
// registration channel's parks on every iteration).
struct SupJob {
    ctx: *SrvCtx,
    m_exec: Chan<(u64, i32)>,
    m_aof: Chan<(u64, i32)>,
    m_sweep: Chan<(u64, i32)>,
}

struct SupSt {
    // Slot arrays, not Vecs: the supervisor is a goroutine,
    // and pushing into a Vec field of a heap struct from a
    // goroutine loop is the measured defect behind probes
    // 18f/18h (see the IoSt comment). Slot 0 is a
    // permanent sentinel (a dummy monitor channel that
    // never delivers); live entries are [1, n); removal
    // swaps the last entry down.
    mons: [Chan<(u64, i32)>; 8192],
    mstreams: [u64; 8192],
    n: u64,
    scan_i: u64,
    conn_traps: u64,
    sweep_restarts: u64,
}

// Executor-local state (Box'd once at executor entry; helpers
// take the pointer so the batch state can be mutated in
// place). The op pool is NOT here any more: it moved up to
// SrvCtx in round-2 step 3, when connection goroutines became
// op producers too.
struct ExecSt {
    ctx: *SrvCtx,
    broken: bool,
    since_yield: u64,
    // The batch being executed and its staged journal records:
    // heap-side because g_aof_append / g_aof_install park this
    // goroutine mid-batch (see ConnJob's note on frame values).
    cur_batch: Vec<Vec<Vec<u8>>>,
    cur_chunks: Vec<Vec<u8>>,
    // Group commit (fsync=always): batches whose append op has
    // been sent but whose covering sync has not landed yet. The
    // done signal for pend_reqs[i] is released by
    // g_recycle_handle when pend_ops[i] is recycled. FIFO: ops
    // are sent in batch order and the writer recycles in the
    // order it processes, so completions arrive head-first.
    pend_reqs: Vec<*CmdReq>,
    pend_ops: Vec<*AofOp>,
}

fn g_signal(req: *CmdReq) {
    if (*req).ack {
        (*req).done.send(1);
    } else {
        select {
            case (*req).done.send(1) {
            }
            default {
            }
        }
    };
}

// ---- store mutex (round-2 step 1) ----
// One mutex serializes the dataset now that connection
// goroutines execute read commands inline. Holders never park
// voluntarily inside the lock: the sections are exec_command,
// store_sweep and dataset_serialize only (pure CPU over the
// store). The blocking form parks the task (not the carrier)
// when contended; probe_mutex (line work dir) measured the
// surface: 39 ns uncontended pair, exact exclusion under
// 8-way contention, waiters park without freezing unrelated
// channel traffic.

fn g_lock_store(ctx: *SrvCtx) {
    let st: i32 = sa_sync_mutex_lock((*ctx).store_mu);
}

fn g_unlock_store(ctx: *SrvCtx) {
    let st: i32 = sa_sync_mutex_unlock((*ctx).store_mu);
}

// Attempt the store lock without parking; true when acquired.
// The inline fast path borrows the frame's parse result, on
// which no park may intervene (see g_try_inline).
fn g_try_lock_store(ctx: *SrvCtx) -> bool {
    let acq: u32 = 0;
    let st: i32 = sa_sync_mutex_try_lock((*ctx).store_mu, &acq);
    return acq != 0;
}

// ---- AOF op pool (SrvCtx side, round-2 step 3) ----
// The pool is shared by every op producer now that connection
// goroutines dispatch their own appends. All accesses hold
// op_mu for a single Vec tail operation; the take's spin
// releases the mutex while yielding, so the mutex itself is
// never a wait point.

fn g_pool_lock(ctx: *SrvCtx) {
    let st: i32 = sa_sync_mutex_lock((*ctx).op_mu);
}

fn g_pool_unlock(ctx: *SrvCtx) {
    let st: i32 = sa_sync_mutex_unlock((*ctx).op_mu);
}

fn g_pool_give(ctx: *SrvCtx, op: *AofOp) {
    g_pool_lock(ctx);
    (*ctx).op_pool.push(op);
    g_pool_unlock(ctx);
}

// Take one op, spinning (yield, never park) while the pool is
// empty. The spin terminates because every op holder makes
// progress without the spinner: the writer returns
// fire-and-forget ops as it cycles, fsync=always waiters
// return their op when its sync lands, and the executor's
// recycle drain restocks group-commit ops. Nobody needs
// op_mu (or any other lock the spinner might hold -- callers
// take ops outside the store lock) to do any of that.
fn g_pool_take(ctx: *SrvCtx) -> *AofOp {
    g_pool_lock(ctx);
    while len((*ctx).op_pool) == 0 {
        g_pool_unlock(ctx);
        let yst: i32 = sa_task_yield();
        g_pool_lock(ctx);
    }
    let n: u64 = len((*ctx).op_pool);
    let op = (*ctx).op_pool[n - 1];
    // Vec has no pop in this stack: rebuild without the tail.
    let nf: Vec<*AofOp> = Vec::new();
    let i: u64 = 0;
    while i + 1 < n {
        nf.push((*ctx).op_pool[i]);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    (*ctx).op_pool = nf;
    g_pool_unlock(ctx);
    return op;
}

// Complete one op exactly once, per the protocol its
// dispatcher armed (see AofOp). Called only by the writer,
// after the op's group has been written and, under
// fsync=always, synced -- i.e. the durability point is the
// writer's sync, exactly where it was when the executor's
// pending FIFO was the only waiter.
fn g_op_complete(ctx: *SrvCtx, op: *AofOp) {
    if (*op).via_recycle {
        (*ctx).aof_recycle.send(op);
    } else {
        if (*op).wait {
            (*op).done.send(1);
        } else {
            g_pool_give(ctx, op);
        };
    };
}

// One recycled op, from whichever consumer met it first: if it
// is the head pending (group-commit) op, its batch's done signal
// is released now -- the covering sync has landed -- and a
// failure marks the executor broken for subsequent batches,
// exactly as the synchronous wait result used to. Either way
// the op returns to the shared pool.
fn g_recycle_handle(st: *ExecSt, op: *AofOp) {
    if len((*st).pend_ops) > 0 {
        if (*(*st).pend_ops[0]).idx == (*op).idx {
            let req = (*st).pend_reqs[0];
            let ok = (*op).result;
            let nr: Vec<*CmdReq> = Vec::new();
            let no: Vec<*AofOp> = Vec::new();
            let i: u64 = 1;
            while i < len((*st).pend_reqs) {
                nr.push((*st).pend_reqs[i]);
                no.push((*st).pend_ops[i]);
                i = i + 1;
            }
            (*st).pend_reqs = nr;
            (*st).pend_ops = no;
            if ok != true {
                (*st).broken = true;
            };
            g_pool_give((*st).ctx, op);
            g_signal(req);
            return;
        };
    };
    g_pool_give((*st).ctx, op);
}

fn g_op_drain(st: *ExecSt) {
    let draining: bool = true;
    while draining {
        select {
            case let op = (*(*st).ctx).aof_recycle.recv() {
                g_recycle_handle(st, op);
            }
            default {
                draining = false;
            }
        }
    }
}

// The executor's take: the plain pool take, but the spin also
// drains the recycle channel -- the executor is the only
// consumer of that channel, and the ops it carries are the
// executor's own group-commit completions, so spinning on the
// pool alone could strand them (and the batches they release)
// behind this very take.
fn g_op_take(st: *ExecSt) -> *AofOp {
    let ctx = (*st).ctx;
    g_op_drain(st);
    g_pool_lock(ctx);
    while len((*ctx).op_pool) == 0 {
        g_pool_unlock(ctx);
        // The pool can only be empty with appends in flight
        // (group commit pipelines them); the writer recycles
        // independently, so yield + drain until one lands.
        // Recycles consumed here complete pending batches via
        // g_recycle_handle, as everywhere else.
        let yst: i32 = sa_task_yield();
        g_op_drain(st);
        g_pool_lock(ctx);
    }
    let n: u64 = len((*ctx).op_pool);
    let op = (*ctx).op_pool[n - 1];
    // Vec has no pop in this stack: rebuild without the tail.
    let nf: Vec<*AofOp> = Vec::new();
    let i: u64 = 0;
    while i + 1 < n {
        nf.push((*ctx).op_pool[i]);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    (*ctx).op_pool = nf;
    g_pool_unlock(ctx);
    return op;
}

// Wait for one op dispatched with wait=true; returns its
// result and returns the op to the pool. The wait PUMPS the
// recycle channel: pending group-commit completions are
// consumed (and their batches released) while this goroutine
// waits, so the writer can never wedge on a full recycle
// channel behind a waiter -- the executor is that channel's
// only consumer, and when it is not in its step select it is
// here. (Connection waiters need no pump: they are leaves --
// nothing the writer or executor waits for depends on a
// connection finishing its wait.)
fn g_op_wait_done(st: *ExecSt, mine: *AofOp) -> bool {
    let ctx = (*st).ctx;
    let waiting: bool = true;
    let res: bool = false;
    while waiting {
        select {
            case let dv = (*mine).done.recv() {
                res = (*mine).result;
                waiting = false;
            }
            case let rop = (*ctx).aof_recycle.recv() {
                g_recycle_handle(st, rop);
            }
            default {
                let yst: i32 = sa_task_yield();
            }
        }
    }
    g_pool_give(ctx, mine);
    return res;
}

// Dispatch one append op carrying a batch's staged journal
// records. Under fsync=always the caller waits for the recycle
// (the records are synced before the batch's replies are
// released, the same durability point the poll loop's inline
// flush produced); other modes are fire-and-forget and learn
// about failures from the writer's sticky flag.
fn g_aof_append(st: *ExecSt, chunks: Vec<Vec<u8>>) -> bool {
    let ctx = (*st).ctx;
    let op = g_op_take(st);
    (*op).kind = AOF_OP_APPEND;
    (*op).chunks = chunks;
    (*op).data = Vec::new();
    (*op).result = false;
    (*op).via_recycle = false;
    if (*ctx).aof_fsync == AOF_FSYNC_ALWAYS {
        (*op).wait = true;
        (*ctx).aof_chan.send(op);
        let ok = g_op_wait_done(st, op);
        if ok != true {
            (*st).broken = true;
        };
        return ok;
    };
    (*op).wait = false;
    (*ctx).aof_chan.send(op);
    return true;
}

// Group-commit form of g_aof_append (fsync=always only): the
// op is dispatched and the batch is parked on the pending FIFO;
// its done signal is released by g_recycle_handle once the
// writer's group sync covering these records lands. The
// executor meanwhile executes later batches, so the records of
// many batches accumulate in the writer's queue and one sync
// covers them all. The durability point is unchanged: no reply
// of this batch is released before a sync covering its records
// completes -- only the waiting is pipelined.
fn g_aof_append_defer(st: *ExecSt, chunks: Vec<Vec<u8>>, req: *CmdReq) {
    let ctx = (*st).ctx;
    let op = g_op_take(st);
    (*op).kind = AOF_OP_APPEND;
    (*op).chunks = chunks;
    (*op).data = Vec::new();
    (*op).result = false;
    (*op).wait = false;
    (*op).via_recycle = true;
    (*ctx).aof_chan.send(op);
    (*st).pend_reqs.push(req);
    (*st).pend_ops.push(op);
}

// Dispatch one install op carrying a serialized dataset. The
// manual BGREWRITEAOF path waits (the poll loop's rewrite was
// synchronous and its reply reports the outcome); the
// writer-requested auto-rewrite install is fire-and-forget.
fn g_aof_install(st: *ExecSt, data: Vec<u8>, wait: bool) -> bool {
    let ctx = (*st).ctx;
    let op = g_op_take(st);
    (*op).kind = AOF_OP_INSTALL;
    (*op).chunks = Vec::new();
    (*op).data = data;
    (*op).result = false;
    (*op).via_recycle = false;
    (*op).wait = wait;
    (*ctx).aof_chan.send(op);
    if wait {
        return g_op_wait_done(st, op);
    };
    return true;
}

// Deep-copy one parsed command's argument vector. The batch
// must own its arguments outright: pr.args is a field of the
// ParseResult value resp_parse returned, and moving that field
// into the batch leaves the buffer double-owned -- the
// parser value's later drop frees arguments the executor is
// still reading (measured: second-command replies containing
// heap debris and "unknown command" fragments). The poll loop
// never moves pr.args (it borrows in place); the clone is the
// goroutine model's equivalent ownership transfer.
fn g_clone_args(args: &Vec<Vec<u8>>) -> Vec<Vec<u8>> {
    let out: Vec<Vec<u8>> = Vec::new();
    let i: u64 = 0;
    while i < len(args) {
        out.push(bytes_clone(&args[i]));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// ---- inline execution (round-2 step 1: lock + inline
// reads; step 3: W1 full inline, writes included) ----
// A command may execute in the connection goroutine, under
// the store mutex, when it is not BGREWRITEAOF. Reads inline
// since step 1. Writes inline since step 3 (the W1 shape,
// DESIGN 3b): the connection executes the write in-lock,
// renders its journal record in-lock, and sends the append
// op on aof_chan WHILE STILL HOLDING the store mutex -- so
// op sends serialize in exactly execution order (AOF record
// order == execution order), the send's stall is bounded
// (the writer drains aof_chan without ever needing the store
// mutex; the channel is buffered; the worst case is one
// writer group cycle), and no reverse lock dependency
// exists (the writer never takes the store mutex). The
// durability gate moves from the executor's pending FIFO to
// the op's own done channel (see AofOp): under fsync=always
// the pass's replies are not emitted until every op the pass
// dispatched has been signalled (g_inline_wait) -- the gate
// itself, "no reply before its covering sync", is unmoved.
// Write effects are visible to other connections' inline
// reads before the sync lands, exactly as in the executor
// form (which also executed first and released replies after
// the sync) and in Redis's appendfsync=always semantics.
// BGREWRITEAOF stays with the executor for its
// serialize/install dance. Every mutating command in the
// dispatch table is a journal write (verified against
// commands.sla's dispatch list), so the write set below is
// exact; anything unrecognized is an exec_command error
// reply, which is equally safe inline.

// Try to execute one parsed command inline, appending its
// reply to the connection's out buffer. Returns false when
// the command must ride the executor batch instead:
//   - BGREWRITEAOF (the executor's dance), or
//   - a batch is already pending for this connection: the
//     batch must execute first (per-connection command order
//     is a wire contract), and flushing it would park this
//     goroutine with the frame's parse result still live --
//     the heap discipline forbids reading it after a park.
//     The command simply joins the batch and the executor
//     runs it in order, or
//   - the try-lock missed (contention): miss-batch-fallback
//     (round-2 step 1, reshaped after the first inline form
//     failed its A/B gate). The command joins the batch like
//     the case above; the batch-nonempty head check then
//     routes every later command of this pass to the batch as
//     well, so the whole remainder of the pass degrades to
//     baseline executor behaviour and per-connection order
//     stays exact. The next pass starts with an empty batch
//     and retries the inline path. The previous fallback
//     shape -- clone the arguments into a heap slot and park
//     on the blocking lock -- serialised contending readers
//     behind ~25 us FIFO lock handoffs and collapsed
//     GET P=16 c50 by -75% (step-1 report); no park may
//     appear in this fallback.
// The execution path that remains:
//   - try-lock acquired: no park has intervened since the
//     parse, so borrowing pr.args (and threading the out
//     buffer through the frame) is sound; this is the poll
//     model's borrow shape plus the lock.
fn g_try_inline(job: *ConnJob, args: &Vec<Vec<u8>>) -> bool {
    if len((*(*job).req).batch) > 0 {
        return false;
    };
    if aof_is_rewrite_command(args) {
        return false;
    };
    if journal_is_write(args) {
        return g_try_inline_write(job, args);
    };
    let ctx = (*job).ctx;
    let now_ms: i64 = sa_time_unix_ms();
    if g_try_lock_store(ctx) {
        let ob = (*job).outbuf;
        (*job).outbuf = exec_command(&(*ctx).store, args, now_ms, ob);
        g_unlock_store(ctx);
        return true;
    };
    return false;
}

// The W1 write half of g_try_inline (journal writes only;
// AOF enabled or not). Mirrors the executor's per-command
// discipline exactly: broken-AOF refusal before execution
// (no record, no store change), the record staged only when
// the reply is not an error, one timestamp shared by the
// execution and its record (the executor samples one per
// batch; inline samples one per command, inside the lock).
fn g_try_inline_write(job: *ConnJob, args: &Vec<Vec<u8>>) -> bool {
    let ctx = (*job).ctx;
    if (*ctx).aof_enabled != true {
        // No journal and no op: a write is a read-shaped
        // inline execution (the record-ordering argument is
        // vacuous with no AOF), same try-lock discipline.
        let now_ms: i64 = sa_time_unix_ms();
        if g_try_lock_store(ctx) {
            let ob = (*job).outbuf;
            (*job).outbuf = exec_command(&(*ctx).store, args, now_ms, ob);
            g_unlock_store(ctx);
            return true;
        };
        return false;
    };
    if (*ctx).aof_broken || (*job).aof_dead {
        // Refused without executing (executor parity: the
        // refusal touches no store state, so it needs no
        // lock; answering inline keeps the pass's reply order
        // exact and costs the failing connection no batch).
        let obe = (*job).outbuf;
        (*job).outbuf = resp_error(obe, STR_PTR("ERR AOF write failed: persistence is broken, writes are refused"), STR_LEN("ERR AOF write failed: persistence is broken, writes are refused"));
        return true;
    };
    // The op is taken BEFORE the store lock: every
    // park-prone pool operation stays outside the lock, and
    // an unused op (try-lock miss, errored command) is simply
    // handed back. Only the send below happens in-lock.
    let op = g_pool_take(ctx);
    if g_try_lock_store(ctx) != true {
        g_pool_give(ctx, op);
        return false;
    };
    let now_ms: i64 = sa_time_unix_ms();
    let rep = exec_command(&(*ctx).store, args, now_ms, Vec::new());
    let is_err: bool = true;
    if len(rep) > 0 {
        if rep[0] != 45 {
            is_err = false;
        };
    };
    if is_err != true {
        let chc: Vec<Vec<u8>> = Vec::new();
        chc.push(journal_record(args, now_ms));
        (*op).kind = AOF_OP_APPEND;
        (*op).chunks = chc;
        (*op).data = Vec::new();
        (*op).result = false;
        (*op).via_recycle = false;
        (*op).wait = (*ctx).aof_fsync == AOF_FSYNC_ALWAYS;
        // THE in-lock send (DESIGN 3b): because this send
        // happens while the store mutex serializes writers,
        // aof_chan order == execution order across every
        // producer (connections here; the executor for its
        // batches, whose sends follow its own in-lock
        // executions of that batch). The auto-rewrite
        // ordering argument also rests on this: a write whose
        // append is queued before the executor's install op
        // has necessarily executed before the serialization
        // that install carries (its lock hold preceded the
        // executor's), so the snapshot includes it; a write
        // queued after lands in the new file. The stall is
        // bounded by one writer group cycle: the writer
        // drains this channel without the store mutex, and
        // the channel is buffered (64) besides.
        (*ctx).aof_chan.send(op);
    };
    g_unlock_store(ctx);
    let ob2 = (*job).outbuf;
    (*job).outbuf = bytes_push_range(ob2, &rep, 0, len(rep));
    if is_err {
        g_pool_give(ctx, op);
        return true;
    };
    if (*ctx).aof_fsync == AOF_FSYNC_ALWAYS {
        (*job).inl_ops.push(op);
        if len((*job).inl_ops) >= INL_WAIT_CAP {
            g_inline_wait(job);
        };
    };
    // Other modes: fire-and-forget -- the writer returns the
    // op to the pool when done with it.
    return true;
}

// Drain this pass's fsync=always sync waits, in send order:
// each op's done fires only after the writer's group sync
// covering its record has landed (or failed). A failure
// latches the connection's aof_dead (the writer also raises
// the ctx sticky flag). Replies accumulated so far are NOT
// emitted before this returns -- g_conn_step calls it ahead
// of the pass-end flush/emit, and INL_WAIT_CAP calls it
// mid-pass -- which is what keeps the durability invariant
// in the W1 shape: a reply is released only after a sync
// covering its record completed. If the peer has meanwhile
// reset the connection, nothing here changes shape: the
// wait is a channel park (the same posture as the old
// batch done wait), the sync lands, the pass finishes, the
// emit drains into a dead out queue the driver discards,
// and the end-of-stream pass closes the connection cleanly.
fn g_inline_wait(job: *ConnJob) {
    let ctx = (*job).ctx;
    let i: u64 = 0;
    while i < len((*job).inl_ops) {
        let op = (*job).inl_ops[i];
        let (dv, dcl) = (*op).done.recv();
        if (*op).result != true {
            (*job).aof_dead = true;
        };
        g_pool_give(ctx, op);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    (*job).inl_ops = Vec::new();
}

// ---- connection goroutine ----

// Send the connection's pending batch to the executor, wait
// for completion, and append the replies to its out buffer in
// order. The batch/reply vectors are reset for the next pass.
fn g_flush_batch(job: *ConnJob) {
    let ctx = (*job).ctx;
    let req = (*job).req;
    if len((*req).batch) == 0 {
        return;
    };
    (*req).kind = CMD_BATCH;
    (*req).ack = true;
    (*ctx).cmd_chan.send(req);
    let (dv, dcl) = (*req).done.recv();
    let ri: u64 = 0;
    while ri < len((*req).replies) {
        let ob = (*job).outbuf;
        (*job).outbuf = bytes_push_range(ob, &(*req).replies[ri], 0, len((*req).replies[ri]));
        ri = ri + 1;
    }
    (*req).batch = Vec::new();
    (*req).replies = Vec::new();
}

// Poke the IO driver's wake socket: one byte on a loopback
// self-connection whose read end sits in the driver's poll
// set, so a poll that would otherwise sleep is interrupted
// the moment reply bytes or a close request are queued. The
// socket is nonblocking; a failed poke only means a wakeup is
// already pending, never a lost one (the queued channel
// message is drained on the next round regardless).
fn g_poke(ctx: *SrvCtx) {
    let one: [u8; 1] = [1u8; 1];
    let op: ptr = PTR_BYTE_ADD(&one, 0);
    let written: u64 = 0;
    let st: i32 = sa_std_net_tcp_stream_write((*ctx).wake_tx, op, 1, &written);
}

// Hand the connection's accumulated reply bytes to the IO
// driver (and wake it). Order on the io_out channel is the
// wire order: bytes always precede a close request sent later.
fn g_emit(job: *ConnJob) {
    if len((*job).outbuf) == 0 {
        return;
    };
    let bytes = (*job).outbuf;
    (*job).outbuf = Vec::new();
    let m = Box::into_raw(Box::new(IoOut { fd: (*job).stream, kind: IO_OUT_BYTES, bytes: bytes }));
    (*(*job).ctx).io_out.send(m);
    g_poke((*job).ctx);
}

fn g_close_ctl(job: *ConnJob, kind: u8) {
    let m = Box::into_raw(Box::new(IoOut { fd: (*job).stream, kind: kind, bytes: Vec::new() }));
    (*(*job).ctx).io_out.send(m);
    g_poke((*job).ctx);
}

fn g_conn_main(job: *ConnJob) {
    // Cross-park state lives in the job block, not in frame
    // locals (the heap discipline in the header comment); the
    // auth config is the job's own copy (see ConnJob.auth_cfg).
    // The loop body only calls g_conn_step: channel operations
    // inside a never-returning loop body leak fixed-stack per
    // iteration on this toolchain (measured, probes 30/31 in
    // the delivery record), so every goroutine's cycle lives
    // in a called function whose frame rewinds on return.
    (*job).authed = (*job).auth_cfg.required != true;
    let alive: bool = true;
    while alive {
        alive = g_conn_step(job);
    }
}

// One connection cycle: wait for one inbox message (a data
// chunk or the end-of-stream marker), run the exec pass over
// everything buffered, flush, emit. Returns false when the
// connection is finished (protocol-error close or the final
// pass after end-of-stream).
fn g_conn_step(job: *ConnJob) -> bool {
        let (msg, mcl) = (*job).inbox.recv();
        let is_eof: bool = (*msg).eof;
        if is_eof != true {
            let mb = (*msg).bytes;
            let bi2: u64 = 0;
            while bi2 < len(mb) {
                (*job).inbuf.push(mb[bi2]);
                bi2 = bi2 + 1;
            }
        };
        // Exec pass over everything buffered (also the final
        // pass on the end-of-stream marker: complete buffered
        // commands still run, exactly as the poll loop executed
        // them on the round that reported the hangup). The AUTH
        // gate and AUTH itself are handled locally, in order; a
        // pending store batch is flushed before either, so
        // reply order matches the poll loop's single out buffer
        // exactly. The AUTH reply and its success bit are
        // computed BEFORE the flush: the flush parks this
        // goroutine, and the parse result (a frame value) is
        // not read after any park.
        while (*job).inoff < len((*job).inbuf) {
            let pr = resp_parse(&(*job).inbuf, (*job).inoff);
            if pr.state == PARSE_INCOMPLETE {
                break;
            };
            if pr.state == PARSE_ERROR {
                // Replies of commands executed before the bad
                // bytes precede the error, as in the poll loop.
                g_flush_batch(job);
                let obe = (*job).outbuf;
                (*job).outbuf = resp_error(obe, STR_PTR("ERR Protocol error"), STR_LEN("ERR Protocol error"));
                (*job).close_after_flush = true;
                (*job).inoff = len((*job).inbuf);
                break;
            };
            (*job).inoff = (*job).inoff + pr.consumed;
            if pr.state == PARSE_OK {
                if auth_gate_allows(&(*job).auth_cfg, (*job).authed, &pr.args) != true {
                    g_flush_batch(job);
                    let obn = (*job).outbuf;
                    (*job).outbuf = resp_error(obn, STR_PTR("NOAUTH Authentication required."), STR_LEN("NOAUTH Authentication required."));
                } else {
                    if auth_is_auth_command(&pr.args) {
                        let arep = cmd_auth(&(*job).auth_cfg, &pr.args, Vec::new());
                        (*job).auth_ok_pending = auth_attempt_succeeds(&(*job).auth_cfg, &pr.args);
                        g_flush_batch(job);
                        let ob2 = (*job).outbuf;
                        (*job).outbuf = bytes_push_range(ob2, &arep, 0, len(arep));
                        if (*job).auth_ok_pending {
                            (*job).authed = true;
                            (*job).auth_ok_pending = false;
                        };
                    } else {
                        if g_try_inline(job, &pr.args) != true {
                            (*(*job).req).batch.push(g_clone_args(&pr.args));
                        };
                    };
                };
                let ye = (*(*job).ctx).yield_every;
                if ye > 0 {
                    (*job).yield_ct = (*job).yield_ct + 1;
                    if (*job).yield_ct >= ye {
                        let yst: i32 = sa_task_yield();
                        (*job).yield_ct = 0;
                    };
                };
            };
        }
        // The pass's inline-write sync waits land before any
        // reply of this pass is emitted (the durability gate,
        // g_inline_wait); the batch flush below keeps its own
        // done wait for whatever rode the executor.
        g_inline_wait(job);
        g_flush_batch(job);
        // compact the input buffer (same rules as the poll loop)
        if (*job).inoff == len((*job).inbuf) {
            (*job).inbuf = Vec::new();
            (*job).inoff = 0;
        } else {
            if (*job).inoff >= 8192 {
                let rest: Vec<u8> = Vec::new();
                let rest2 = bytes_push_range(rest, &(*job).inbuf, (*job).inoff, len((*job).inbuf));
                (*job).inbuf = rest2;
                (*job).inoff = 0;
            };
        };
        g_emit(job);
        if (*job).close_after_flush {
            g_close_ctl(job, IO_OUT_CLOSE_AFTER_FLUSH);
            return false;
        };
        if is_eof {
            g_close_ctl(job, IO_OUT_CLOSE_AFTER_FLUSH);
            return false;
        };
        return true;
}

// ---- executor goroutine ----

// Run one connection batch against the store. Mirrors the
// poll loop's conn_exec_all decision chain minus the AUTH steps
// (those never leave the connection goroutine): BGREWRITEAOF,
// the broken-AOF write refusal, normal dispatch, and the
// journal hook -- with records staged per batch and dispatched
// to the AOF writer once, before the batch is signalled done,
// so a reply never precedes its record's handoff.
// Execute one batch; returns true when its done signal was
// deferred to the group-commit pending FIFO (fsync=always with
// journal records staged), false when the caller signals now.
fn g_exec_batch(st: *ExecSt, req: *CmdReq) -> bool {
    let ctx = (*st).ctx;
    if (*ctx).aof_broken {
        (*st).broken = true;
    };
    let now_ms: i64 = sa_time_unix_ms();
    (*req).replies = Vec::new();
    (*st).cur_chunks = Vec::new();
    // The batch vector moves out of the request block into the
    // executor state block; the sender reinitializes it after
    // the done signal.
    (*st).cur_batch = (*req).batch;
    let bi: u64 = 0;
    while bi < len((*st).cur_batch) {
        if aof_is_rewrite_command(&(*st).cur_batch[bi]) {
            if len((*st).cur_batch[bi]) != 1 {
                (*req).replies.push(err_arity(Vec::new(), STR_PTR("bgrewriteaof"), STR_LEN("bgrewriteaof")));
            } else {
                if (*ctx).aof_enabled != true {
                    (*req).replies.push(resp_error(Vec::new(), STR_PTR("ERR BGREWRITEAOF requires AOF persistence to be enabled"), STR_LEN("ERR BGREWRITEAOF requires AOF persistence to be enabled")));
                } else {
                    // This batch's earlier records belong to the
                    // pre-rewrite file: the append op is FIFO-
                    // ordered ahead of the install op, so the
                    // writer lands them before the rename (the
                    // poll loop flushed its staged records
                    // inside aof_rewrite before serializing).
                    if len((*st).cur_chunks) > 0 {
                        let chc = (*st).cur_chunks;
                        (*st).cur_chunks = Vec::new();
                        let okc = g_aof_append(st, chc);
                    };
                    g_lock_store(ctx);
                    let data = dataset_serialize(&(*ctx).store, now_ms, Vec::new());
                    g_unlock_store(ctx);
                    if g_aof_install(st, data, true) {
                        (*req).replies.push(resp_simple(Vec::new(), STR_PTR("AOF rewrite completed"), STR_LEN("AOF rewrite completed")));
                    } else {
                        (*req).replies.push(resp_error(Vec::new(), STR_PTR("ERR AOF rewrite failed"), STR_LEN("ERR AOF rewrite failed")));
                    };
                };
            };
        } else {
            // Write classification once per command, as in the
            // poll loop (F-perf-4 rationale there applies here).
            let is_write: bool = false;
            if (*ctx).aof_enabled {
                is_write = journal_is_write(&(*st).cur_batch[bi]);
            };
            if is_write && (*st).broken {
                (*req).replies.push(resp_error(Vec::new(), STR_PTR("ERR AOF write failed: persistence is broken, writes are refused"), STR_LEN("ERR AOF write failed: persistence is broken, writes are refused")));
            } else {
                g_lock_store(ctx);
                let rep = exec_command(&(*ctx).store, &(*st).cur_batch[bi], now_ms, Vec::new());
                if is_write {
                    if len(rep) > 0 {
                        if rep[0] != 45 {
                            (*st).cur_chunks.push(journal_record(&(*st).cur_batch[bi], now_ms));
                        };
                    };
                };
                g_unlock_store(ctx);
                (*req).replies.push(rep);
            };
        };
        bi = bi + 1;
        if (*ctx).yield_every > 0 {
            (*st).since_yield = (*st).since_yield + 1;
            if (*st).since_yield >= (*ctx).yield_every {
                let yst: i32 = sa_task_yield();
                (*st).since_yield = 0;
            };
        };
    }
    if len((*st).cur_chunks) > 0 {
        let chc2 = (*st).cur_chunks;
        (*st).cur_chunks = Vec::new();
        if (*ctx).aof_fsync == AOF_FSYNC_ALWAYS {
            // Group commit: defer this batch's done signal
            // until the covering sync lands (see
            // g_aof_append_defer); the caller (g_exec_handle)
            // must not signal.
            g_aof_append_defer(st, chc2, req);
            (*st).cur_batch = Vec::new();
            return true;
        };
        let okf = g_aof_append(st, chc2);
    };
    (*st).cur_batch = Vec::new();
    return false;
}

fn g_exec_main(job: *ExecJob) {
    let ctx = (*job).ctx;
    let st = Box::into_raw(Box::new(ExecSt { ctx: ctx, broken: false, since_yield: 0, cur_batch: Vec::new(), cur_chunks: Vec::new(), pend_reqs: Vec::new(), pend_ops: Vec::new() }));
    // The op pool lives in SrvCtx (created in
    // run_server_goroutine before any goroutine spawns): it
    // is shared with the connection goroutines since step 3.
    // Loop body is a single call (the channel-op discipline
    // at g_conn_main): the recv lives in g_exec_step.
    while true {
        g_exec_step(st);
    }
}

// Dispatch one request taken off the command channel (sweep /
// serialize / batch). A batch whose done signal was deferred
// (group commit) is signalled later by g_recycle_handle.
fn g_exec_handle(st: *ExecSt, req: *CmdReq) {
        let ctx = (*st).ctx;
        if (*req).kind == CMD_SWEEP {
            g_lock_store(ctx);
            let swept: u64 = store_sweep(&(*ctx).store, sa_time_unix_ms());
            g_unlock_store(ctx);
            g_signal(req);
        } else {
            if (*req).kind == CMD_SERIALIZE {
                // Writer-requested auto-rewrite: serialize on
                // the executor (the store owner), hand the
                // payload to the writer as an install op. FIFO
                // order on both channels is the whole ordering
                // argument: appends sent before this install
                // reflect commands the snapshot already
                // includes... and appends sent after it carry
                // post-snapshot records into the new file.
                g_lock_store(ctx);
                let data = dataset_serialize(&(*ctx).store, sa_time_unix_ms(), Vec::new());
                g_unlock_store(ctx);
                let oki = g_aof_install(st, data, false);
                g_signal(req);
            } else {
                let deferred = g_exec_batch(st, req);
                if deferred != true {
                    g_signal(req);
                };
            };
        };
}

// One executor cycle: take one request off the command
// channel and dispatch it (sweep / serialize / batch). While
// group-commit batches are pending, the cycle also listens on
// the recycle channel: a pending batch completes there. (With
// nothing pending the cycle is the historical blocking recv --
// the recycle channel is drained opportunistically by
// g_op_take / g_op_drain on the paths that need ops.)
fn g_exec_step(st: *ExecSt) {
        let ctx = (*st).ctx;
        if len((*st).pend_ops) == 0 {
            let (req, cl) = (*ctx).cmd_chan.recv();
            g_exec_handle(st, req);
        } else {
            select {
                case let req = (*ctx).cmd_chan.recv() {
                    g_exec_handle(st, req);
                }
                case let op = (*ctx).aof_recycle.recv() {
                    g_recycle_handle(st, op);
                }
            }
        };
}

// ---- AOF writer goroutine ----
// Owns the AofState and the file handle. Append ops are
// written (and synced, per policy) in channel order through
// the same aof_flush_pending the poll loop uses; install ops
// swap in executor-serialized datasets through aof_swap_in.
// The 100 ms tick drives the everysec fsync (aof_tick_sync)
// and the auto-rewrite trigger, which asks the executor for a
// serialization instead of touching the store itself.
fn g_aof_main(job: *AofJob) {
    // Loop body is a single call (the channel-op discipline
    // at g_conn_main): the select lives in g_aof_step.
    while true {
        g_aof_step(job);
    }
}

// One writer cycle: one op off the AOF channel, or the
// 100 ms tick (everysec sync + auto-rewrite trigger). An
// append op takes any further appends already queued with it
// (group commit): each op's chunks are written in channel
// order, then under fsync=always ONE sync covers the group.
// An install op stops the group: the grouped appends flush
// (and sync) first, then the install runs, preserving the
// FIFO ordering argument end to end.
fn g_aof_step(job: *AofJob) {
        let ctx = (*job).ctx;
        select {
            case let op = (*ctx).aof_chan.recv() {
                if (*op).kind == AOF_OP_INSTALL {
                    if (*job).wst.broken {
                        (*op).result = false;
                    } else {
                        (*op).result = aof_swap_in(&(*job).wst, &(*op).data, sa_time_unix_ms());
                    };
                    (*job).rw_out = false;
                    if (*job).wst.broken {
                        (*ctx).aof_broken = true;
                    };
                    g_op_complete(ctx, op);
                } else {
                    let group: Vec<*AofOp> = Vec::new();
                    group.push(op);
                    let install_op = group[0];
                    let has_install: bool = false;
                    let any_write: bool = false;
                    let write_failed: bool = false;
                    // Step 2 (group write): COLLECT the whole
                    // drained group first, merging every op's
                    // record chunks into the job's gbuf, then pay
                    // ONE aof_write_group for the group instead
                    // of one aof_write_all call chain per record.
                    // Arrivals during the write form the next
                    // group; the group cap (256) and the install
                    // truncation are unchanged.
                    let draining: bool = true;
                    while draining {
                        if len(group) >= 256 {
                            draining = false;
                        } else {
                            select {
                                case let op2 = (*ctx).aof_chan.recv() {
                                    if (*op2).kind == AOF_OP_INSTALL {
                                        install_op = op2;
                                        has_install = true;
                                        draining = false;
                                    } else {
                                        group.push(op2);
                                    };
                                }
                                default {
                                    draining = false;
                                }
                            }
                        };
                    }
                    let ci2: u64 = 0;
                    while ci2 < len(group) {
                        let cop = group[ci2];
                        let chi: u64 = 0;
                        while chi < len((*cop).chunks) {
                            let gb = (*job).gbuf;
                            (*job).gbuf = bytes_push_range(gb, &(*cop).chunks[chi], 0, len((*cop).chunks[chi]));
                            chi = chi + 1;
                        }
                        (*cop).chunks = Vec::new();
                        ci2 = ci2 + 1;
                    }
                    if (*job).wst.broken {
                        write_failed = true;
                        let bi: u64 = 0;
                        while bi < len(group) {
                            let bop = group[bi];
                            (*bop).result = false;
                            bi = bi + 1;
                        }
                    } else {
                        let wok = aof_write_group(&(*job).wst, &(*job).gbuf);
                        let wi: u64 = 0;
                        while wi < len(group) {
                            let wop = group[wi];
                            (*wop).result = wok;
                            wi = wi + 1;
                        }
                        if wok {
                            any_write = true;
                        } else {
                            write_failed = true;
                        };
                    };
                    (*job).gbuf = Vec::new();
                    if (*ctx).aof_fsync == AOF_FSYNC_ALWAYS {
                        if any_write && write_failed != true {
                            let sok = aof_sync_now(&(*job).wst, sa_time_unix_ms());
                            if sok != true {
                                write_failed = true;
                            };
                        };
                        if write_failed {
                            let fi: u64 = 0;
                            while fi < len(group) {
                                let fop = group[fi];
                                (*fop).result = false;
                                fi = fi + 1;
                            }
                        };
                    };
                    if (*job).wst.broken {
                        (*ctx).aof_broken = true;
                    };
                    // Completion (round-2 step 3 protocol,
                    // g_op_complete): done-signals and pool
                    // returns first -- they can never block --
                    // then the executor's recycle feed, whose
                    // sends may wait for the executor to drain
                    // (it does: step select, op-take drain, or
                    // the wait pump). Every op's result was
                    // set above, before any completion is
                    // signalled, so no waiter can observe a
                    // half-completed group.
                    let ri: u64 = 0;
                    while ri < len(group) {
                        let rop = group[ri];
                        if (*rop).via_recycle != true {
                            g_op_complete(ctx, rop);
                        };
                        ri = ri + 1;
                    }
                    let rj: u64 = 0;
                    while rj < len(group) {
                        let rop2 = group[rj];
                        if (*rop2).via_recycle {
                            g_op_complete(ctx, rop2);
                        };
                        rj = rj + 1;
                    }
                    if has_install {
                        if (*job).wst.broken {
                            (*install_op).result = false;
                        } else {
                            (*install_op).result = aof_swap_in(&(*job).wst, &(*install_op).data, sa_time_unix_ms());
                        };
                        (*job).rw_out = false;
                        if (*job).wst.broken {
                            (*ctx).aof_broken = true;
                        };
                        g_op_complete(ctx, install_op);
                    };
                };
            }
            timeout(100000000) {
                let now_ms: i64 = sa_time_unix_ms();
                if (*job).wst.broken != true {
                    let oks = aof_tick_sync(&(*job).wst, now_ms);
                    if (*job).wst.broken {
                        (*ctx).aof_broken = true;
                    };
                    if (*job).rw_out != true {
                        if (*job).wst.size >= (*job).wst.base_size + AOF_REWRITE_MIN_GROWTH {
                            if (*job).wst.size >= (*job).wst.base_size * 2 {
                                (*job).rw_out = true;
                                let sreq = (*job).sreq;
                                (*sreq).kind = CMD_SERIALIZE;
                                (*ctx).cmd_chan.send(sreq);
                            };
                        };
                    };
                };
            }
        }
}

// ---- expiry sweeper goroutine ----
// The poll loop swept inside its round at a ~100 ms cadence;
// here a dedicated goroutine posts a sweep request to the
// executor at the same cadence (the store is only ever touched
// by the executor, sweeps included).
fn g_sweep_main(job: *SweepJob) {
    // Loop body is a single call (the channel-op discipline
    // at g_conn_main): the send lives in g_sweep_step.
    while true {
        g_sweep_step(job);
    }
}

fn g_sweep_step(job: *SweepJob) {
        let ctx = (*job).ctx;
        let req = (*job).req;
        let sst: i32 = sa_task_sleep_ms(100);
        (*req).kind = CMD_SWEEP;
        (*ctx).cmd_chan.send(req);
}

// ---- IO driver (runs on the main goroutine's frame) ----
// Exactly one execution context touches sockets in this
// model: the driver. It owns the listener and every
// connection socket, waits on all of them with the readiness
// face (in POLL_CHUNK-sized waits, the poll model's staging
// discipline), accepts, reads chunks into per-connection
// channels, and writes out queues fed by the connection
// goroutines over the io_out channel.
//
// Why the driver sits on the main frame (measured; probes
// 8/11/12/13/14 and 24/24b/24c in the delivery record): on
// the current runtime, blocking net waits in spawned
// goroutines do not compose -- once one goroutine is parked
// in a blocking read, a second goroutine's blocking read on
// another socket is starved indefinitely (its data is
// delivered only after the first waiter leaves), a
// goroutine's second blocking accept park poisons other
// waiters the same way, and calling the readiness wait from
// a spawned goroutine starves channel delivery
// process-wide. The same readiness wait called from the
// main goroutine composes (it is the poll model's seat).
// Channel waits and sleeps compose fine anywhere. So all
// net waits are concentrated in the driver on the main
// frame, and every other goroutine parks on channels. The
// runtime defects are registered for the compiler line;
// this is the model shape that fits the runtime as
// measured.
//
// The driver's own state is heap-side (IoSt), and its table
// holds pointers to per-connection blocks, so retiring a
// connection never moves another connection's bytes.
struct IoSt {
    ctx: *SrvCtx,
    listener: u64,
    wake_rx: u64,
    // The connection table is a fixed slot array, not a Vec:
    // pushing into a Vec field of a heap struct from inside a
    // goroutine loop is a measured compiler/runtime defect on
    // this toolchain (goroutine stack overflow abort; minimal
    // repro probes 18f/18h in the delivery record). Index
    // assignment into an array field is sound. The cap is far
    // above the acceptance envelope (1200); at the cap the
    // driver simply leaves new connections in the listen
    // backlog for a later round.
    slots: [*DriverConn; 8192],
    n: u64,
    lis_ready: u32,
    wake_ready: u32,
    any_dead: bool,
}

// Deep-copy the shared auth config for one connection (the
// password bytes are cloned; the config is read-only after
// startup, so the copy can never go stale).
fn g_clone_auth(ap: *AuthConfig) -> AuthConfig {
    // The source is the shared, startup-frozen config: read
    // its password field in place, never bind it to a local --
    // binding a Vec field of a heap struct MOVES the field out
    // (the codebase's take idiom), which would drain the
    // shared password for every later connection (measured:
    // first connection AUTHs, all later ones WRONGPASS).
    let pw: Vec<u8> = Vec::new();
    let i: u64 = 0;
    while i < len((*ap).password) {
        pw.push((*ap).password[i]);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return AuthConfig { required: (*ap).required, password: pw };
}

fn g_io_index(st: *IoSt, fd: u64) -> (bool, u64) {
    let i: u64 = 0;
    while i < (*st).n {
        if (*(*st).slots[i]).fd == fd {
            return (true, i);
        };
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return (false, 0);
}

fn g_io_close(st: *IoSt, dc: *DriverConn) {
    if (*dc).dead != true {
        let cc: i32 = sa_std_close((*dc).fd);
        (*dc).dead = true;
        (*st).any_dead = true;
    };
}

// Deliver the end-of-stream marker to a connection goroutine
// (once, and only when its inbox has room -- a full inbox
// means the goroutine is mid-dispatch; the marker is retried
// on later rounds until it lands).
fn g_io_send_eof(dc: *DriverConn) {
    if (*dc).eof_sent {
        return;
    };
    if (*dc).inbox.len() >= 16 {
        return;
    };
    let m = Box::into_raw(Box::new(IoMsg { bytes: Vec::new(), eof: true }));
    (*dc).inbox.send(m);
    (*dc).eof_sent = true;
}

// Apply every queued io_out message: reply bytes join the
// connection's out queue; close requests act in channel
// order, so bytes queued before a close are flushed first.
fn g_io_drain_out(st: *IoSt) {
    let ctx = (*st).ctx;
    let pending: Vec<*IoOut> = Vec::new();
    let draining: bool = true;
    while draining {
        select {
            case let m = (*ctx).io_out.recv() {
                pending.push(m);
            }
            default {
                draining = false;
            }
        }
    }
    let pi: u64 = 0;
    while pi < len(pending) {
        let m = pending[pi];
        let (found, idx) = g_io_index(st, (*m).fd);
        if found {
            let dc = (*st).slots[idx];
            if (*dc).dead != true {
                if (*m).kind == IO_OUT_BYTES {
                    let mb = (*m).bytes;
                    let oq = (*dc).outq;
                    (*dc).outq = bytes_push_range(oq, &mb, 0, len(mb));
                } else {
                    if (*m).kind == IO_OUT_CLOSE_NOW {
                        g_io_close(st, dc);
                    } else {
                        (*dc).close_after_flush = true;
                        if (*dc).out_off >= len((*dc).outq) {
                            g_io_close(st, dc);
                        };
                    };
                };
            };
        };
        pi = pi + 1;
    }
}

fn g_io_wait(st: *IoSt) -> bool {
    let hs: [u64; 128] = [0u64; 128];
    let ins: [u32; 128] = [0u32; 128];
    let rds: [u32; 128] = [0u32; 128];
    let hp: ptr = PTR_BYTE_ADD(&hs, 0);
    let ip: ptr = PTR_BYTE_ADD(&ins, 0);
    let rp: ptr = PTR_BYTE_ADD(&rds, 0);
    (*st).lis_ready = 0;
    (*st).wake_ready = 0;
    let ready_count: u64 = 0;
    let waited: bool = false;
    let next_i: u64 = 0;
    let phase: u64 = 0;
    let total: u64 = (*st).n + 2;
    while phase * 128 < total {
        // stage one chunk: slot order is listener, wake,
        // then connections in table order
        let n: u64 = 0;
        let chunk_start: u64 = phase * 128;
        let slot: u64 = chunk_start;
        while n < 128 {
            if slot >= total {
                break;
            };
            if slot == 0 {
                hs[n] = (*st).listener;
                ins[n] = SA_NET_POLL_READ;
            } else {
                if slot == 1 {
                    hs[n] = (*st).wake_rx;
                    ins[n] = SA_NET_POLL_READ;
                } else {
                    let dc = (*st).slots[slot - 2];
                    hs[n] = (*dc).fd;
                    let it: u32 = 0;
                    if (*dc).read_closed != true {
                        it = it + SA_NET_POLL_READ;
                    };
                    if (*dc).out_off < len((*dc).outq) {
                        it = it + SA_NET_POLL_WRITE;
                    };
                    ins[n] = it;
                };
            };
            n = n + 1;
            slot = slot + 1;
        }
        let tmo: i32 = 0;
        if waited != true {
            tmo = 50;
        };
        let pst: i32 = sa_std_net_poll_wait(hp, ip, n, tmo, rp, &ready_count);
        waited = true;
        if pst != SA_STD_OK {
            return false;
        };
        // scatter bits back over the same slot sequence
        let k: u64 = 0;
        let slot2: u64 = chunk_start;
        while k < n {
            if slot2 == 0 {
                (*st).lis_ready = rds[k];
            } else {
                if slot2 == 1 {
                    (*st).wake_ready = rds[k];
                } else {
                    (*(*st).slots[slot2 - 2]).ready = rds[k];
                };
            };
            k = k + 1;
            slot2 = slot2 + 1;
        }
        phase = phase + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// Accept every pending connection (bounded per round, as in
// the poll model): each gets a nonblocking socket owned by
// this driver, a DriverConn block, an inbox channel, and its
// own connection goroutine, registered with the supervisor.
fn g_io_accept(st: *IoSt) {
    let ctx = (*st).ctx;
    let accepts: u64 = 0;
    while accepts < 64 {
        if (*st).n >= 8192 {
            break;
        };
        let h: u64 = 0;
        let ast: i32 = sa_std_net_tcp_accept((*st).listener, &h);
        if ast != SA_STD_OK {
            break;
        };
        if h == 0 {
            break;
        };
        let nb: i32 = sa_std_net_tcp_stream_set_nonblocking(h, 1);
        if nb != SA_STD_OK {
            let cc0: i32 = sa_std_close(h);
            accepts = accepts + 1;
        } else {
            let inbox: Chan<*IoMsg> = Chan::new(16);
            let dc = Box::into_raw(Box::new(DriverConn { fd: h, inbox: inbox, outq: Vec::new(), out_off: 0, ready: 0, read_closed: false, eof_sent: false, close_after_flush: false, dead: false }));
            if (*st).n < 8192 {
                (*st).slots[(*st).n] = dc;
                (*st).n = (*st).n + 1;
            };
            let dchan: Chan<u64> = Chan::new(0);
            let req = Box::into_raw(Box::new(CmdReq { kind: CMD_BATCH, ack: true, batch: Vec::new(), replies: Vec::new(), done: dchan }));
            let cj = Box::into_raw(Box::new(ConnJob { ctx: ctx, auth_cfg: g_clone_auth((*ctx).auth), stream: h, req: req, inbox: inbox, inbuf: Vec::new(), inoff: 0, outbuf: Vec::new(), inl_args: Vec::new(), inl_ops: Vec::new(), aof_dead: false, authed: false, close_after_flush: false, yield_ct: 0, auth_ok_pending: false }));
            let gh = go g_conn_main(cj);
            (*ctx).sup_reg.send((gh, h));
            accepts = accepts + 1;
        };
    }
}

// Read at most one chunk from a ready connection and hand it
// to its goroutine; a full inbox applies backpressure (the
// bytes stay in the socket; readiness is level-triggered).
// EOF and fatal read errors close the read side and deliver
// the end-of-stream marker.
fn g_io_read(st: *IoSt, dc: *DriverConn) {
    if (*dc).read_closed {
        return;
    };
    if (*dc).inbox.len() >= 16 {
        return;
    };
    let buf: [u8; 4096] = [0u8; 4096];
    let bp: ptr = PTR_BYTE_ADD(&buf, 0);
    let nread: u64 = 0;
    let rst: i32 = sa_std_net_tcp_stream_read((*dc).fd, bp, 4096, &nread);
    if rst != SA_STD_OK {
        // would-block on a nonblocking socket, or a real
        // error; the ERROR readiness bit distinguishes a dead
        // socket from a spurious wakeup
        if ((*dc).ready & SA_NET_POLL_ERROR) != 0 {
            (*dc).read_closed = true;
        };
        return;
    };
    if nread == 0 {
        (*dc).read_closed = true;
        return;
    };
    let data: Vec<u8> = Vec::new();
    let j: u64 = 0;
    while j < nread {
        data.push(buf[j]);
        j = j + 1;
    }
    let m = Box::into_raw(Box::new(IoMsg { bytes: data, eof: false }));
    (*dc).inbox.send(m);
}

// One write attempt on a connection with queued output (the
// poll model's conn_flush, one 4 KiB staged chunk): partial
// progress is kept; a drained queue triggers a pending
// close-after-flush; a hard error closes the connection and
// notifies its goroutine.
fn g_io_write(st: *IoSt, dc: *DriverConn) {
    if (*dc).out_off >= len((*dc).outq) {
        return;
    };
    let avail: u64 = len((*dc).outq) - (*dc).out_off;
    let chunk: u64 = avail;
    if chunk > 4096 {
        chunk = 4096;
    };
    let arr: [u8; 4096] = [0u8; 4096];
    let j: u64 = 0;
    while j < chunk {
        arr[j] = (*dc).outq[(*dc).out_off + j];
        j = j + 1;
    }
    let ap: ptr = PTR_BYTE_ADD(&arr, 0);
    let written: u64 = 0;
    let wst: i32 = sa_std_net_tcp_stream_write((*dc).fd, ap, chunk, &written);
    if wst != SA_STD_OK {
        if ((*dc).ready & SA_NET_POLL_ERROR) != 0 {
            g_io_close(st, dc);
            (*dc).read_closed = true;
            g_io_send_eof(dc);
        };
        return;
    };
    if written == 0 {
        return;
    };
    (*dc).out_off = (*dc).out_off + written;
    if (*dc).out_off == len((*dc).outq) {
        (*dc).outq = Vec::new();
        (*dc).out_off = 0;
        if (*dc).close_after_flush {
            g_io_close(st, dc);
        };
    };
}

fn g_io_run(job: *IoJob) -> i32 {
    let ctx = (*job).ctx;
    let dummy_inbox: Chan<*IoMsg> = Chan::new(1);
    let sentinel = Box::into_raw(Box::new(DriverConn { fd: 0, inbox: dummy_inbox, outq: Vec::new(), out_off: 0, ready: 0, read_closed: true, eof_sent: true, close_after_flush: false, dead: true }));
    let st = Box::into_raw(Box::new(IoSt { ctx: ctx, listener: (*job).listener, wake_rx: (*job).wake_rx, slots: [sentinel; 8192], n: 0, lis_ready: 0, wake_ready: 0, any_dead: false }));
    while (*ctx).core_dead != true {
        g_io_drain_out(st);
        if g_io_wait(st) {
            if (*st).wake_ready != 0 {
                // drain the wake socket (its bytes carry no
                // information; one pending byte wakes a round)
                let wbuf: [u8; 64] = [0u8; 64];
                let wp: ptr = PTR_BYTE_ADD(&wbuf, 0);
                let wn: u64 = 0;
                let wst2: i32 = sa_std_net_tcp_stream_read((*st).wake_rx, wp, 64, &wn);
            };
            if ((*st).lis_ready & SA_NET_POLL_READ) != 0 {
                g_io_accept(st);
            };
            let ci: u64 = 0;
            while ci < (*st).n {
                let dc = (*st).slots[ci];
                if (*dc).dead != true {
                    if ((*dc).ready & (SA_NET_POLL_READ + SA_NET_POLL_ERROR)) != 0 {
                        g_io_read(st, dc);
                    };
                    if (*dc).read_closed {
                        g_io_send_eof(dc);
                    };
                    if (*dc).dead != true {
                        g_io_write(st, dc);
                    };
                };
                ci = ci + 1;
            }
        };
        if (*st).any_dead {
            // swap-remove the dead slots (pointer moves only;
            // order in the table carries no semantics)
            let ki: u64 = 0;
            while ki < (*st).n {
                if (*(*st).slots[ki]).dead {
                    (*st).n = (*st).n - 1;
                    (*st).slots[ki] = (*st).slots[(*st).n];
                } else {
                    ki = ki + 1;
                };
            }
            (*st).any_dead = false;
        };
    }
    return G_EXIT_CORE_DEAD;
}

// ---- entry point + supervisor ----
// Startup mirrors run_server (server.sla): replay + open the
// AOF before listening (any failure exits 69), listen (66),
// listener nonblocking setup (67). The frame then becomes the
// supervisor instead of an event loop. Exit codes 65/66/69
// are shared with the poll model; 67 in this model also
// covers the wake-socket setup; 71 is new (core goroutine
// death: executor, AOF writer, or IO driver gone -- see the
// header comment).
fn run_server_goroutine(store: Store, host: Vec<u8>, port: u32, auth: AuthConfig, aof_cfg: AofConfig) -> i32 {
    if len(host) > 63 {
        return 65;
    };
    // The AOF state is built and opened in this frame first
    // (exactly the poll model's startup): calling aof_open
    // through a borrow of an already-boxed state segfaults at
    // startup on this toolchain (measured; staged bisection
    // in the delivery record). After startup the state moves
    // into the AOF writer's job block as an owned value (see
    // AofJob.wst).
    let aof = aof_state_new(&aof_cfg);
    if aof.enabled {
        let rc: i32 = aof_replay(&aof_cfg, &store, sa_time_unix_ms());
        if rc != 0 {
            return 69;
        };
        if aof_open(&aof) != true {
            return 69;
        };
        aof.last_sync_ms = sa_time_unix_ms();
    };
    let aof_enabled_v: bool = aof.enabled;
    let aof_fsync_v: u8 = aof.fsync_mode;
    let harr: [u8; 64] = [0u8; 64];
    let hi: u64 = 0;
    while hi < len(host) {
        harr[hi] = host[hi];
        hi = hi + 1;
    }
    let hp: ptr = PTR_BYTE_ADD(&harr, 0);
    let listener: u64 = 0;
    let bound: u32 = 0;
    let lst: i32 = sa_std_net_tcp_listen(hp, len(host), port, &listener, &bound);
    if lst != SA_STD_OK {
        return 66;
    };
    let lnb: i32 = sa_std_net_tcp_listener_set_nonblocking(listener, 1);
    if lnb != SA_STD_OK {
        return 67;
    };
    // Wake channel for the IO driver: a loopback
    // self-connection whose read end sits in the driver's poll
    // set, so queued replies wake it immediately.
    let wl: u64 = 0;
    let wbound: u32 = 0;
    let wst0: i32 = sa_std_net_tcp_listen(STR_PTR("127.0.0.1"), 9, 0, &wl, &wbound);
    if wst0 != SA_STD_OK {
        return 67;
    };
    let wake_tx: u64 = 0;
    let wct: i32 = sa_std_net_tcp_connect(STR_PTR("127.0.0.1"), 9, wbound, &wake_tx);
    if wct != SA_STD_OK {
        return 67;
    };
    let wake_rx: u64 = 0;
    let wat: i32 = sa_std_net_tcp_accept(wl, &wake_rx);
    if wat != SA_STD_OK {
        return 67;
    };
    let wcl: i32 = sa_std_close(wl);
    let wnb1: i32 = sa_std_net_tcp_stream_set_nonblocking(wake_tx, 1);
    if wnb1 != SA_STD_OK {
        return 67;
    };
    let wnb2: i32 = sa_std_net_tcp_stream_set_nonblocking(wake_rx, 1);
    if wnb2 != SA_STD_OK {
        return 67;
    };
    // Manual yield cadence for this module's CPU loops (bench
    // knob; absent or invalid keeps the default 128).
    let yield_every: u64 = 128;
    let ye = env_value(STR_PTR("SLAREDIS_YIELD_EVERY"), STR_LEN("SLAREDIS_YIELD_EVERY"));
    if ye.present {
        let (ok_y, v_y) = parse_i64_full(&ye.value);
        if ok_y && v_y >= 0 {
            yield_every = v_y as u64;
        };
    };
    let cmd_chan: Chan<*CmdReq> = Chan::new(256);
    let aof_chan: Chan<*AofOp> = Chan::new(64);
    let aof_recycle: Chan<*AofOp> = Chan::new(64);
    let sup_reg: Chan<(u64, u64)> = Chan::new(256);
    let io_out: Chan<*IoOut> = Chan::new(256);
    let authp = Box::into_raw(Box::new(auth));
    // The store mutex (round-2 step 1): created before the
    // context exists; a creation failure is a setup failure,
    // reported with the setup exit family (67).
    let store_mu: u64 = 0;
    let mst: i32 = sa_sync_mutex_new(&store_mu);
    if mst != 0 {
        return 67;
    };
    // The AOF op pool (round-2 step 3): preallocated here,
    // before any goroutine exists, so no producer ever
    // allocates an op at run time. Sized for group commit:
    // under fsync=always many appends are in flight at once
    // (one per unreleased write across all connections, one
    // per pending executor batch), where a purely synchronous
    // world needed one.
    let op_mu: u64 = 0;
    let mst2: i32 = sa_sync_mutex_new(&op_mu);
    if mst2 != 0 {
        return 67;
    };
    let op_pool: Vec<*AofOp> = Vec::new();
    let pi: u64 = 0;
    while pi < 256 {
        let dch: Chan<u64> = Chan::new(1);
        let opx = Box::into_raw(Box::new(AofOp { kind: AOF_OP_APPEND, idx: pi, chunks: Vec::new(), data: Vec::new(), result: false, done: dch, wait: false, via_recycle: false }));
        op_pool.push(opx);
        pi = pi + 1;
    }
    let ctx = Box::into_raw(Box::new(SrvCtx {
        auth: authp,
        aof_enabled: aof_enabled_v,
        aof_fsync: aof_fsync_v,
        store: store,
        store_mu: store_mu,
        op_pool: op_pool,
        op_mu: op_mu,
        cmd_chan: cmd_chan,
        aof_chan: aof_chan,
        aof_recycle: aof_recycle,
        sup_reg: sup_reg,
        io_out: io_out,
        wake_tx: wake_tx,
        core_dead: false,
        aof_broken: false,
        yield_every: yield_every,
    }));
    // Spawn order: consumers first, the IO driver last, so no
    // connection can be accepted before its executor exists.
    let ej = Box::into_raw(Box::new(ExecJob { ctx: ctx }));
    let h_exec = go g_exec_main(ej);
    let m_exec = monitor(h_exec);
    let m_aof: Chan<(u64, i32)> = Chan::new(0);
    if aof_enabled_v {
        let sd: Chan<u64> = Chan::new(1);
        let sreq = Box::into_raw(Box::new(CmdReq { kind: CMD_SERIALIZE, ack: false, batch: Vec::new(), replies: Vec::new(), done: sd }));
        let aj = Box::into_raw(Box::new(AofJob { ctx: ctx, wst: aof, gbuf: Vec::new(), sreq: sreq, rw_out: false }));
        let h_aof = go g_aof_main(aj);
        m_aof = monitor(h_aof);
    };
    let swd: Chan<u64> = Chan::new(1);
    let swreq = Box::into_raw(Box::new(CmdReq { kind: CMD_SWEEP, ack: false, batch: Vec::new(), replies: Vec::new(), done: swd }));
    let swj = Box::into_raw(Box::new(SweepJob { ctx: ctx, req: swreq }));
    let h_sweep = go g_sweep_main(swj);
    let m_sweep = monitor(h_sweep);
    let sj = Box::into_raw(Box::new(SupJob { ctx: ctx, m_exec: m_exec, m_aof: m_aof, m_sweep: m_sweep }));
    let h_sup = go g_sup_main(sj);
    // The IO driver runs on this (main) frame, not in a
    // spawned goroutine: sa_std_net_poll_wait called from a
    // spawned goroutine starves channel delivery for the
    // other goroutines (measured; probes 24/24b/24c in the
    // delivery record), while from the main goroutine -- the
    // poll model's own seat -- it composes. The supervisor is
    // a goroutine instead; it only ever parks on channels.
    let ioj = Box::into_raw(Box::new(IoJob { ctx: ctx, listener: listener, wake_rx: wake_rx }));
    return g_io_run(ioj);
}

// The supervisor goroutine: owns every monitor channel and
// the sweeper's respawn; never touches sockets or the store.
fn g_sup_main(sj: *SupJob) {
    let ctx = (*sj).ctx;
    let dummy_mon: Chan<(u64, i32)> = Chan::new(1);
    // Supervisor loop: one select whose arms are the
    // connection-monitor watch (round-robin over the table),
    // the core monitors (executor, AOF writer -- any exit is
    // process-fatal by design and is reported through
    // ctx.core_dead), the sweeper monitor (respawned), and
    // sup_reg registrations; a 20 ms timeout sets the idle
    // cadence. See the one-select constraint at the loop.
    // Slot 0 is a permanent sentinel: the dummy monitor
    // channel (never delivers), so the select's watch arm
    // always has a valid table entry even with no live
    // connections; real registrations append from slot 1.
    let sup = Box::into_raw(Box::new(SupSt { mons: [dummy_mon; 8192], mstreams: [0u64; 8192], n: 1, scan_i: 0, conn_traps: 0, sweep_restarts: 0 }));
    // Loop body is a single call (the channel-op discipline
    // at g_conn_main): the whole cycle lives in g_sup_step,
    // whose frame rewinds on return. The step carries the
    // current sweeper monitor in (*sj).m_sweep (it changes
    // on respawn).
    let alive: bool = true;
    while alive {
        alive = g_sup_step(sup, sj);
    }
}

// One supervisor cycle: one select (all waits as arms),
// then the actions for whichever arm fired. Returns false
// when a core goroutine has exited (the caller then
// returns, ending the supervisor; ctx.core_dead is set).
fn g_sup_step(sup: *SupSt, sj: *SupJob) -> bool {
        let ctx = (*sj).ctx;
        let m_exec = (*sj).m_exec;
        let m_aof = (*sj).m_aof;
    // The select merges every wait into one statement, and
    // the whole cycle lives in this called function -- two
    // measured runtime constraints (probes 27/28/30/31 in
    // the delivery record): (1) channel operations
    // (send/recv/select) textually inside a never-returning
    // loop body leak fixed-stack per iteration -- a plain
    // recv in a loop body dies within thousands of
    // iterations, a select faster in proportion to its arm
    // count (the five-select form of this loop died at ~643
    // iterations) -- while the same operations inside a
    // CALLED function rewind on return (300k calls clean);
    // (2) arm order is the priority order under the
    // runtime's declaration-order bias: connection-exit
    // first (rarest, hygiene), core-death next, sweeper,
    // registrations last (frequent; they cannot starve the
    // rare arms, and when only registrations are ready the
    // arm fires immediately, so bursts drain at full speed).
    // The connection monitor arm watches ONE table entry per
    // cycle in round-robin (the watched channel is chosen
    // before the select), so a trapped connection is retired
    // within n cycles -- seconds at the 8192 cap, ample for
    // a hygiene path.
        if (*sup).scan_i >= (*sup).n {
            (*sup).scan_i = 0;
        };
        let watch_idx = (*sup).scan_i;
        let watch = (*sup).mons[watch_idx];
        (*sup).scan_i = (*sup).scan_i + 1;
        let got_reg: bool = false;
        let regh: u64 = 0;
        let regfd: u64 = 0;
        let dead: bool = false;
        let sweep_dead: bool = false;
        let exit_found: bool = false;
        let exit_code: i32 = 0;
        select {
            case let mc = watch.recv() {
                exit_found = true;
                exit_code = mc.1;
            }
            case let m1 = m_exec.recv() {
                dead = true;
            }
            case let m2 = m_aof.recv() {
                dead = true;
            }
            case let m4 = (*sj).m_sweep.recv() {
                sweep_dead = true;
            }
            case let reg = (*ctx).sup_reg.recv() {
                got_reg = true;
                regh = reg.0;
                regfd = reg.1;
            }
            timeout(20000000) {
            }
        }
        if got_reg {
            if (*sup).n < 8192 {
                (*sup).mons[(*sup).n] = monitor(regh);
                (*sup).mstreams[(*sup).n] = regfd;
                (*sup).n = (*sup).n + 1;
            };
        };
        if dead {
            // a core goroutine is gone: flag it; the main
            // frame's IO driver notices within one round and
            // returns G_EXIT_CORE_DEAD for the process
            (*ctx).core_dead = true;
            return false;
        };
        if sweep_dead {
            (*sup).sweep_restarts = (*sup).sweep_restarts + 1;
            let swd2: Chan<u64> = Chan::new(1);
            let swreq2 = Box::into_raw(Box::new(CmdReq { kind: CMD_SWEEP, ack: false, batch: Vec::new(), replies: Vec::new(), done: swd2 }));
            let swj2 = Box::into_raw(Box::new(SweepJob { ctx: ctx, req: swreq2 }));
            let h_sweep2 = go g_sweep_main(swj2);
            (*sj).m_sweep = monitor(h_sweep2);
        };
        if exit_found {
            if exit_code != 0 {
                // A trapped connection goroutine may have left
                // its socket half-served; sockets belong to
                // the IO driver, so the close goes through it.
                (*sup).conn_traps = (*sup).conn_traps + 1;
                let cm = Box::into_raw(Box::new(IoOut { fd: (*sup).mstreams[watch_idx], kind: IO_OUT_CLOSE_NOW, bytes: Vec::new() }));
                (*ctx).io_out.send(cm);
                g_poke(ctx);
            };
            (*sup).n = (*sup).n - 1;
            (*sup).mons[watch_idx] = (*sup).mons[(*sup).n];
            (*sup).mstreams[watch_idx] = (*sup).mstreams[(*sup).n];
        };
        return true;
}
